LINE RULES (within one contiguous removed-run/added-run pair inside a hunk -- what unified diff
already calls a "replace region": a solid run of `-` lines immediately followed by a solid run
of `+` lines)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Comment and blank lines are handled OUTSIDE the pairing machinery entirely:
  * A REMOVED line that is itself a `//`, `///`, `//!` comment, or blank/whitespace-only, is
    ALWAYS `P-inert` -- independent of whatever it is "paired" with. Comment CONTENT changes
    (a doc comment's wording changing) are never inspected; only whether the line as a whole is
    a comment/blank line.
  * An ADDED line that is a comment/blank AND is not itself absorbed as the partner of a
    substantive removed line (see `V-commented-out` below) is `P-inert`.

For every remaining (non-comment, non-blank) removed line R and added line A in the same
replace region, pairing is decided by GLOBAL greedy maximum similarity:
  1. Compute `difflib.SequenceMatcher(None, R, A).ratio()` for every candidate (R, A) pair still
     unconsumed in this replace region.
  2. Repeatedly consume the single highest-ratio pair, provided that ratio is >= the acceptance
     threshold (0.55). Stop when no remaining pair clears the threshold, or one side is empty.
  3. A removed line left unconsumed after step 2 is a `V-unpaired-removal` -- it lost its pin
     with no legitimate extension partner in the diff at all (this is what a naive positional
     zip, or a naive "closest available regardless of how weak the match is" pairing, both
     PERMIT -- see `bite-14-pairing-shift-attack`'s header comment for the exact shape).
  4. An added line left unconsumed after step 2, in a hunk that ALSO contains an unpaired
     removal, needs no separate classification -- the hunk already BITEs via
     `V-unpaired-removal`. Emit no P-* claim for it. `V-added-weakener` (see below) is checked
     independently regardless of pairing.
  5. An added line left unconsumed after step 2, in a hunk with NO unpaired removal (e.g. this
     replace region's every removed line found a partner, but there were still-more added
     lines), falls to the file-class rule for a pure/unmatched addition (EXISTING-TEST:
     permitted; EXISTING-SRC: `V-unpermitted-src` unless comment/blank).

MULTI-LINE (RUSTFMT-WRAPPED) LITERALS. Before the pairing above runs, if a replace region has M
removed lines and N added lines with M != N, and joining ALL of one side's lines (strip each
line's leading/trailing whitespace, join with a single space) turns it into a single logical
statement whose shape plausibly matches the OTHER (unjoined) side's single line (same rule
applies after joining: same P-idset / P-count shape test), collapse that side to ONE logical
line for the purposes of every rule below. This is what makes a `[u32; N] = [...]` literal that
rustfmt wraps across 3 lines (`[`, the values, `];`) PERMIT identically to its single-line form.

For each PAIRED (removed, added) line (after any multi-line join), try each rule below IN
ORDER; the first that matches wins and is the sole reason recorded for that pair:

  P-content-version -- both sides match `CONTENT_VERSION: u32 = <int>;` (whitespace-insensitive,
    the exact declaration form used in `server-module/src/lib.rs`) and the added integer is >=
    the removed integer.

  P-count -- define a line's SKELETON as the line with every maximal run of ASCII digits NOT
    inside a `[...]` bracket replaced by a single `#`. Both sides' skeletons (after this
    replacement) are textually identical, AND every digit run in that position increased or
    stayed equal between removed and added, AND at least one such run strictly increased. This
    is the `paths.len() == 10` -> `== 12` / `.len(), 9,` -> `.len(), 9,`-with-a-changed-neighbour
    shape: a bare pinned integer literal (test assertion argument, `assert_eq!` 2nd arg, etc.),
    never one embedded inside a `[...]` id list (that is P-idset's job) and never one embedded
    inside a `"..."` string literal (that is P-msg-or-name's job, see below).

  P-idset -- the line contains exactly one `[...]` bracketed, comma-separated list of unsigned
    integers (a Rust array/slice literal or a `BTreeSet`/`HashSet`-collected `[...]`). The added
    side's list, as a SET, is a strict superset of the removed side's list as a set (every
    removed element present in the added list, and at least one element present that was not in
    the removed list). The line's SKELETON outside the bracketed list (and outside any adjoining
    `[TYPE; N]` array-length annotation immediately preceding the list, whose `N` is elided
    entirely from the skeleton comparison) must be textually identical between removed and
    added. A mismatched `[TYPE; N]` length (N not equal to the new list's cardinality) is a Rust
    compile error the specialist's own `cargo build` catches; this tool does not special-case it
    as a distinct violation.

  P-range-to-list -- the removed side contains a Rust inclusive-range-then-collect expression
    matching `(<int>..=<int>).collect()` (whitespace-insensitive). The added side contains an
    explicit `vec![...]` (or bare `[...]`) integer list whose elements, as a set, are a superset
    of `expand(a..=b)` (every integer from the removed range's `a` through `b` inclusive is
    present in the added list).

  P-msg-or-name -- ONLY inside a file of class EXISTING-TEST (never EXISTING-SRC). The removed
    and added lines are identical after (a) erasing the contents of every `"..."` double-quoted
    string literal on the line, and (b) erasing the identifier immediately following the token
    `fn ` up to the next `(`. AND no bare (non-string, non-`fn`-name) integer literal on the
    line decreased. AND the line, on either side, introduces none of the `V-weakened` tokens
    listed below. This is the `fn t2_..._ten_edges` -> `..._twelve_edges` rename, and an
    `assert_eq!` message-string rewrite, shape.

If NO P-* rule matches a paired (removed, added) line:
  * If the ADDED side of the pair is a comment/blank and the REMOVED side is substantive ->
    `V-commented-out`.
  * Else if the added side introduces `>=`, `<=`, `is_subset`, `is_superset`, `debug_assert`,
    `debug_assert_eq`, `debug_assert_ne`, the literal token sequence `|| true`, or the literal
    token sequence `&& false`, where the removed side did not already contain that same token ->
    `V-weakened`.
  * Else if the removed side contains `assert_eq!` or `assert_ne!` and the added side's first
    non-whitespace token is `assert!` (a macro downgrade) -> `V-weakened`.
  * Else, for a pair that is STILL recognizably the same kind of pinning assertion (same macro
    name, same base identifier chain e.g. `paths.len()` / `levels_by_species.len()` / a
    `BTreeSet`/array id-list literal) but whose bound has shrunk in a way P-count/P-idset did
    not accept (a dropped id, a lowered integer) -> `V-weakened`. (RULES has no separate
    `V-idset` or `V-count` violation id -- a shrunk pin on an otherwise-still-present assertion
    is `V-weakened`, not a distinct id; `V-weakened` is the general "this paired, still-present
    assertion got looser" bucket, not merely the six textual tokens above.)
  * Else, in EXISTING-SRC -> `V-unpermitted-src`. In EXISTING-TEST, this residual case does not
    arise given the buckets above; if it somehow does, treat as `V-weakened` (fail loud rather
    than silently PERMIT).

Independent of pairing, ANY added line anywhere in a Rust-file hunk (paired, unpaired, or inside
an otherwise-PERMITted pure-addition hunk) that is, once leading whitespace is stripped, exactly
`#[ignore]`, or matches `#[cfg(feature = "..."` (any feature-gate attribute), is
`V-added-weakener` regardless of any other classification that line's hunk received.

`V-test-count` is a WHOLE-FILE, cross-hunk, cross-diff aggregate check, run ONCE per file that
classifies as EXISTING-TEST (never for a NEW-TEST file -- this is what stops a slice's own new
test file from laundering a deletion elsewhere in the SAME diff by making the global sum look
non-negative): count lines, across every hunk touching that file, that are exactly `#[test]`
(after stripping whitespace) on the removed side, and the same on the added side. If
added_count < removed_count for that file, emit exactly one `("V-test-count", path, detail)`
reason. This count is NEVER summed across files.

`V-banned-path` and `V-new-non-test-rs` and the NEW-TEST/NON-RUST wholesale PERMITs are decided
purely from a file's path and its old/new existence (`--- /dev/null` vs `+++ /dev/null`), before
any line-level analysis of that file's hunks runs at all.

VERDICT: "BITE" iff `reasons` contains at least one entry whose rule_id is in `VIOLATION_RULES`;
otherwise "PERMIT". `reasons` MUST be populated with every P-* match and every V-* match this
run made (not only violations) -- the selftest battery's per-rule coverage assertion depends on
inspecting a PERMIT fixture's fired P-* rule ids, not merely on the PERMIT/BITE verdict.
